<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.1: Analyzing Two Sets of Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02008</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.1: Analyzing Two Sets of Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>940a8468-4315-4ca2-9ef9-ab1c6eb56791</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para id="para-00001"><emphasis>Set 1:</emphasis></para>
<para id="para-00002">\(f(x)=x^2+4x\)</para>
<para id="para-00003">\(g(x)=x(x+4)\)</para>
<para id="para-00004">\(h(x)=(x+2)^2−4\)</para>
<para id="para-00005"><emphasis>Set 2:</emphasis></para>
<para id="para-00006">\(p(x)=-x^2+6x−5\)</para>
<para id="para-00007">\(q(x)=(5−x)(x−1)\)</para>
<para id="para-00008">\(r(x)=-1(x−3)^2+4\)</para>

<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00009">After observing the two sets, what do you notice?</para>
    <para id="para-00010">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00011">Compare your Answer:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00012">Things you notice:</para>
  
  <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Each set has three equations.</item>
    <item>Each equation has the output in function notation on the left side of the equal sign, and an expression on the right side.</item>
    <item>The expressions are in standard form, factored form, and one other form.</item>
    <item>The equations in standard and factored form are equivalent.</item>
  </list>

</solution></exercise>

<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    
    <para id="para-00013">After observing the two sets, what do you wonder?</para>
    <para id="para-00014">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>s</emphasis><emphasis>olution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
  <para id="para-00015">Compare your Answer:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00016">Things you may wonder:</para>
  
  <list id="list-00002">
    <item>Why are the equations in each set grouped together?</item>
    <item>In each set, is the expression in the last equation written in a particular form? What is it called?</item>
    <item>In each set, is the expression in the last equation equivalent to the other two?</item>
  </list>

</solution></exercise>
</section>
</content></document>